29	    /// Provides a code action to sort the imports and exports in the file using a built-in or custom order.
30	    ///
31	    /// Imports and exports are first separated into chunks, before being sorted.
32	    /// Imports or exports of a chunk are then grouped according to the user-defined groups.
33	    /// Within a group, imports are sorted using a built-in order that depends on the import/export kind, whether the import/export has attributes and the source being imported from.
34	    /// **source** is also often called **specifier** in the JavaScript ecosystem.
35	    ///
36	    /// ```js,ignore
37	    /// import A from "@my/lib" with { "attribute1": "value" };
38	    /// ^^^^^^^^       ^^^^^^^         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
39	    ///   kind         source                attributes
40	    ///
41	    /// export * from "@my/lib" with { "attribute1": "value" };
42	    /// ^^^^^^^^       ^^^^^^^         ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
43	    ///   kind         source                attributes
44	    /// ```
45	    ///
46	    ///
47	    /// ## Chunk of imports and chunk of exports
48	    ///
49	    /// A **chunk** is a sequence of adjacent imports or exports.
50	    /// A chunk contains only imports or exports, not both at the same time.
51	    /// The following example includes two chunks.
52	    /// The first chunk consists of the three imports and the second chunk consists of the three exports.
53	    ///
54	    /// ```js,ignore
55	    /// // chunk 1
56	    /// import A from "a";
57	    /// import * as B from "b";
58	    /// import { C } from "c";
59	    /// // chunk 2
60	    /// export * from "d";
61	    /// export * as F from "e";
62	    /// export { F } from "f";
63	    /// ```
64	    ///
65	    /// Chunks also end as soon as a statement or a **side-effect import** (also called _bare import_) is encountered.
66	    /// Every side-effect import forms an independent chunk.
67	    /// The following example contains six chunks:
68	    ///
69	    /// ```js,ignore
70	    /// // chunk 1
71	    /// import A from "a";
72	    /// import * as B from "b";
73	    /// // chunk 2
74	    /// import "x";
75	    /// // chunk 3
76	    /// import "y";
77	    /// // chunk 4
78	    /// import { C } from "c";
79	    /// // chunk 5
80	    /// export * from "d";
81	    /// function f() {}
82	    /// // chunk 6
83	    /// export * as E from "e";
84	    /// export { F } from "f";
85	    /// ```
86	    ///
87	    /// 1. The first chunk contains the two first `import` and ends with the appearance of the first side-effect import `import "x"`.
88	    /// 2. The second chunk contains only the side-effect import `import "x"`.
89	    /// 3. The third chunk contains only the side-effect import `import "y"`.
90	    /// 4. The fourth chunk contains a single `import`; The first `export` ends it.
91	    /// 5. The fifth chunk contains the first `export`; The function declaration ends it.
92	    /// 6. The sixth chunk contains the last two `export`.
93	    ///
94	    /// Chunks are also delimited by detached comments.
95	    /// A **detached comment** is a comment followed by a blank line.
96	    /// Comments not followed by a blank line are **attached comments**.
97	    /// Note that blank lines alone are not taken into account when chunking imports and exports.
98	    /// The following example contains a detached comment that splits the imports into two chunks:
99	    ///
100	    /// ```js,ignore
101	    /// // Attached comment 1
102	    /// import A from "a";
103	    ///
104	    /// // Attached comment 2
105	    /// import * as B from "b";
106	    /// // Detached comment
107	    ///
108	    /// import { C } from "c";
109	    /// ```
110	    ///
111	    /// The line `import { C } from "c"` forms the second chunk.
112	    /// The blank line between the first two imports is ignored so they form a single chunk.
113	    ///
114	    /// The sorter ensures that chunks are separated from each other with blank lines.
115	    /// Only side-effect imports adjacent to a chunk of imports are not separated by a blank line.
116	    /// The following code...
117	    ///
118	    /// ```js,ignore
119	    /// import A from "a";
120	    /// import * as B from "b";
121	    /// import "x";
122	    /// import { C } from "c";
123	    /// export * from "d";
124	    /// // Detached comment
125	    ///
126	    /// export * as F from "e";
127	    /// // Attached comment
128	    /// export { F } from "f";
129	    /// ```
130	    ///
131	    /// is sorted as:
132	    ///
133	    /// ```js,ignore
134	    /// import A from "a";
135	    /// import * as B from "b";
136	    /// import "x";
137	    /// import { C } from "c";
138	    ///
139	    /// export * from "d";
140	    ///
141	    /// // Detached comment
142	    ///
143	    /// export * as F from "e";
144	    /// // Attached comment
145	    /// export { F } from "f";
146	    /// ```
147	    ///
148	    /// Also, note that blank lines inside a chunk are ignored and preserved.
149	    /// They can be removed by explicitly defining groups as demonstrated in the next section.
150	    ///
151	    ///
152	    /// ## Import and export sorting
153	    ///
154	    /// Once chunks are formed, imports and exports of each chunk are sorted.
155	    /// Imports and exports are sorted by their source.
156	    /// Sources are ordered by "distance".
157	    /// Sources that are "farther" from the current module are put on the top, sources "closer" to the user are put on the bottom.
158	    /// This leads to the following order:
159	    ///
160	    /// 1. URLs such as `https://example.org`.
161	    /// 2. Packages with a protocol such as `node:path`, `bun:test`, `jsr:@my?lib`, or `npm:lib`.
162	    /// 3. Packages such as `mylib` or `@my/lib`.
163	    /// 4. Aliases: sources starting with `@/`, `#`, `~`, `$`, or `%`.
164	    ///    They usually are [Node.js subpath imports](https://nodejs.org/api/packages.html#subpath-imports) or [TypeScript path aliases](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#paths).
165	    /// 5. Absolute and relative paths.
166	    ///
167	    /// Two imports/exports with the same source category are sorted using a [natural sort order](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_sort_order) tailored to URLs, packages, and paths.
168	    /// Notably, the order ensures that `A < a < B < b`.
169	    /// The order takes also numbers into account, e.g. `a9 < a10`.
170	    ///
171	    /// For example, the following code...
172	    ///
173	    /// ```js,ignore
174	    /// import sibling from "./file.js";
175	    /// import internal from "#alias";
176	    /// import fs from "fs";
177	    /// import { test } from "node:test";
178	    /// import path from "node:path";
179	    /// import parent from "../parent.js";
180	    /// import scopedLibUsingJsr from "jsr:@scoped/lib";
181	    /// import data from "https://example.org";
182	    /// 
